" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the origin"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333041" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333041" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\lambda }_{2}<0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }_{2}=0"] ["math" "{\\lambda }_{2}>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333041" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\lambda }_{2}<0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c675f557abede7c8f21eadf3fd65dbd522c5ee9ad0a4ed0a804b03f357c1dc3b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a25c57a021e42b2fa78b71f9ab79f0676a16f7c4f1082af0e9502d4d63ed4e46.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}<{\\lambda }_{2}<0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/state.json" "file_sha256" "b604e3cf3d1bb5fd9774f1ad83835d7bda57d58ad3023b5a271ef3b40f64451c"} {"question" "q-333328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b20be943601aa019f404b7f365efd0336dcd5697c2bf0e37d0e29a3fef87be38"} {"questio