rkers/differential/14069584/example-21632.json" "file_sha256" "d367c542a3c563f9e65112ffd265878f1424033468bd52f4e59be464c84899f4"} {"question" "e-21635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/example-21635.json" "file_sha256" "749b5552a5bc9a3298c0ccd5f4d80d194e0b5037c0444687b05cfbe9e4db16e3"} {"question" "e-21635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/7282a63a66c9f449844292764e4e7b6f1ec472a47ff18aa7fd0c68dbe6042dbe.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/9804740137aa14411a15e6a8fce4d495527bdc1980dbd0cda294cfcf05e89dd8.png)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/b06ebf2f3fa8f0616092292e746e84c7e925c42d38cf1c31dc4f6edede6d001b.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/44c8b9978f9c0db5fc39702755042f1887a95fb2034bb6b01dd8d025b589aba5.png)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/example-21635.json" "file_sha256" "749b5552a5bc9a3298c0ccd5f4d80d194e0b5037c0444687b05cfbe9e4db16e3"} {"question" "e-21634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/example-21634.json" "file_sha256" "645a269904e0898e40c79084279612b2d894d1c7f09d5c30d2c439f9d009d69c"} {"question" "e-21634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1c9e5558e6c3c4a97881100ad5a36c5ab6b2c602eb521123371bdde9c8450a6e.png)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a14a160428831f0fdf8a22ebcb932e655046fe4c4e258bc0d83b25c97ecfafbb.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c2114a7edccbf2b296cca7e1cb9c08cd29ba063745a00d55de2ed06026aa2060.png)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/01186c121850d622e0f4216bb6375f7ef11d2d4cef0dd2acf2eb8f99d2a16643.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/0a96347bbf236e77b719686bfe2cb275ad9a4cc1061edc1dacf3a70be423d9fc.png)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/example-21634.json" "file_sha256" "645a269904e0898e40c79084279612b2d894d1c7f09d5c30d2c439f9d009d69c"}] "usage" {"e-21635" [] "e-21632" [] "e-21634" [] "q-333646" []} "basis" 2385 "retractions" [[17592186167177 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-4$ and $λ_{2} =-1$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n$y = \\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}."] [17592186166473 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - 3y(t)$\nSketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations above given that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$, respectively."] [17592186166473 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/a3ea75c2afeb553be2bd71c6717e1fa0.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/cb8af2968caaacb5b676cea6316430ee.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n$\\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1 < 0$\nthus,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nis the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/ccc3466bc4de9ad91d2f893096827841.png)"] [17592186166476 :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T$, respectively.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/7f0b26ad72c30f42ceeabc3bf1096678.png)\n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$, and $0$."] [17592186166476 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/3f9d87b005f31a0a928364a190e4d763.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 > 0$.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= 0$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/a75b5bab1a5310e66dfd91a6e1bae33a.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0$.\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2$.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0$."] [17592186166475 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 9x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) =-6x(t) - 6y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T$, respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)."] [17592186166475 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -\\dfrac12x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-2x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/0e06d98a83065e6228e2e9238e34f00e.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164462 17592186164463 17592186164464 17592186164466 17592186164467 17592186164468 17592186164469 17592186164471 17592186164472 17592186164474 17592186164475] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164461 :answer-field/id] [17592186164461 :answer-field/key] [17592186164461 :answer-field/type] [17592186164465 :answer-field/id] [17592186164465 :answer-field/key] [17592186164465 :answer-field/type] [17592186164470 :answer-field/id] [17592186164470 :answer-field/key] [17592186164470 :answer-field/type] [17592186164473 :answer-field/id] [17592186164473 :answer-field/key] [17592186164473 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
